  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Comprehensive Reintegration Programme in Areas of Returns in Burundi (Phase IV)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In May 2020, Burundi held its first general elections since 2015. During the previous electoral cycle, political instability, coupled with natural hazards, forced more than 267,000 Burundians to flee the country and resulted in the internal displacement of 202,000 persons. Since the inauguration of the new government in June 2020, the country has witnessed notable improvements in terms of its security, political, and socioeconomic situation. These improved conditions have contributed to the voluntary return of a considerable number of Burundian refugees from neighbouring countries to their communities of origin, with more and more Burundians continuing to express their willingness to return home. 
 
To contribute to national efforts in strengthening reintegration capacity for Burundian returnees and displaced populations, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is providing reintegration assistance to approximately 44,085 individuals, including returnees, host community members and other displaced persons. This is being achieved through improving access to information on stability in areas of return through the Stability Index, improving access to basic services and livelihoods opportunities through small grants mechanisms and livelihoods programming, delivery of direct assistance and services to meet protection, water, sanitation and hygiene, shelter, mental health and psychosocial needs.</narrative>
  </description>
